  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday morning through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 301 PM EDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nAs the long term begins, the region is positioned between a \ndeveloping cut-off low over the Ozarks and a shifting Bermuda high \noff the eastern seaboard. This will set up a southerly flow over the \nCWA and feed ample Gulf moisture into the region through the period. \nAs a result, we will see an increase of scattered to numerous \nthunderstorms by Friday afternoon into the weekend. \n\nAdditionally, precipitable water values increase precipitously on \nFriday, with values spiking up to 2.25 inches favoring heavy \nrainfall rates with more organized storms during the afternoon. As a \nresult, the WPC has the northern and western portions of our area in \na marginal risk for excessive rainfall, so we will need to monitor \nconditions as they develop. With the increase of rain chances, it is \nworth noting that nearly 80% of the state is at least abnormally \ndry, so many locations will benefit from the additional rainfall. \nOtherwise, with a saturated column and lack of meaningful shear, the \nrisk of severe weather is low outside of the daily CAPE variability \nsupporting a few strong storms and gusty winds as the primary \nthreat. \n\nWith a warmer fetch of Gulf air and moisture, we can expect a \ngeneral increase in our daily temperatures into early next week. \nExpect temperatures to climb into the upper 80s to low 90s outside \nof the mountain counties. One limiting aspect, will be more \nexpansive cloud cover that could keep high temperatures down a \ncouple of degrees, but temperatures will still be generally above \naverage through the period. Heat index values will again be up near \nthe triple-digit mark over the central and eastern counties through \nthe weekend, but likely remaining just below Heat Advisory Criteria.",
